{
  "note": "Referred Pain",
  "source": "5559a9f045e1",
  "script": "865c4911a7a3",
  "voice": "Patrick - Engaging and Warm",
  "model": "eleven_v4",
  "fingerprint": "de0e733b07a3",
  "duration": 151.38,
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Referred Pain",
      "start": 0,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Referred pain is pain that arises in the internal organs but is perceived instead on the surface of the body.",
          "start": 0,
          "end": 6.72
        },
        {
          "text": "We'll look first at the convergence theory proposed to explain it, and then at the classic examples of where each organ's pain is felt.",
          "start": 6.76,
          "end": 14.08
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Convergence theory",
      "start": 14.916,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "The proposed mechanism is known as convergence.",
          "start": 14.916,
          "end": 18.356
        },
        {
          "text": "Nociceptors from several locations, both visceral and somatic, converge on a single ascending tract in the spinal cord.",
          "start": 18.396,
          "end": 25.996
        },
        {
          "text": "In other words, a primary sensory neuron from an organ and one from a region of skin synapse on the same secondary sensory neuron.",
          "start": 26.036,
          "end": 34.409
        },
        {
          "text": "From there, the ascending path of that secondary neuron runs up to the somatosensory cortex.",
          "start": 34.463,
          "end": 40.316
        },
        {
          "text": "Now, pain signals from the skin are more common than pain from the internal organs.",
          "start": 40.356,
          "end": 45.196
        },
        {
          "text": "For that reason, the brain associates activation of that pathway with pain in the skin.",
          "start": 45.236,
          "end": 50.476
        },
        {
          "text": "As a result, the visceral signal is felt on the body surface rather than in the organ itself.",
          "start": 50.516,
          "end": 56.196
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Classic examples",
      "start": 56.973,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Turning to the classic examples, the heart refers pain to the chest and the left arm.",
          "start": 56.973,
          "end": 62.28
        },
        {
          "text": "Pain from the liver and the gall bladder is referred to the shoulder region.",
          "start": 62.306,
          "end": 66.293
        },
        {
          "text": "Next, the stomach refers pain to the upper central abdomen.",
          "start": 66.333,
          "end": 70.2
        },
        {
          "text": "The small intestine refers to the umbilical region, while the colon refers to the lower central abdomen.",
          "start": 70.226,
          "end": 76.36
        },
        {
          "text": "The appendix refers to the periumbilical region.",
          "start": 76.386,
          "end": 79.453
        },
        {
          "text": "Finally, the ureters refer to the groin.",
          "start": 79.773,
          "end": 82.453
        },
        {
          "text": "In each case, the organ and its referral site share the same level of entry into the spinal cord.",
          "start": 82.493,
          "end": 88.453
        },
        {
          "text": "That shared level is what allows their nociceptors to converge.",
          "start": 88.493,
          "end": 92.853
        },
        {
          "text": "One further point came up in class, beyond the slides.",
          "start": 92.893,
          "end": 96.12
        },
        {
          "text": "The classic cardiac pattern of chest and left arm pain comes mostly from studies in men.",
          "start": 96.146,
          "end": 101.613
        },
        {
          "text": "Women having a heart attack more often report less typical distributions of pain.",
          "start": 101.733,
          "end": 106.28
        },
        {
          "text": "It's a reminder that much of our knowledge of pain carries a male bias.",
          "start": 106.306,
          "end": 110.813
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Summary",
      "start": 111.569,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "In summary, referred pain is visceral pain that is perceived on the body surface.",
          "start": 111.569,
          "end": 117.009
        },
        {
          "text": "Visceral and somatic nociceptors converge on the same ascending tract in the spinal cord.",
          "start": 117.089,
          "end": 122.396
        },
        {
          "text": "Because cutaneous pain is more common, the brain attributes the signal to the skin.",
          "start": 122.502,
          "end": 127.209
        },
        {
          "text": "As for the classic referrals, the heart refers to the chest and left arm, and the liver and gall bladder to the shoulder region.",
          "start": 127.249,
          "end": 134.582
        },
        {
          "text": "The stomach, small intestine and colon refer to successive bands of the central abdomen.",
          "start": 134.636,
          "end": 140.129
        },
        {
          "text": "Lastly, the appendix refers to the periumbilical region, and the ureters to the groin.",
          "start": 140.369,
          "end": 145.649
        },
        {
          "text": "And that's referred pain, a signal from the organs that the brain reads as coming from the skin.",
          "start": 145.729,
          "end": 151.409
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
